# =============================================================================
# The frontend's entire configuration surface
# =============================================================================
#
# Every variable this interface reads is listed here. `src/lib/env.ts` is the
# single read site for the public ones and `src/lib/serverEnv.ts` for the one
# server-only one, so this file and those two modules are the whole story.
#
# NOTHING HERE HAS A DEFAULT, and that is deliberate. Each reader reports absence
# honestly and its consumer decides what absence means, because the answers
# genuinely differ — an unset API base is a working fixture deployment, while an
# unset RPC url means a wallet cannot be offered the chain. Copy this to
# `.env.local` and set what you need.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The backend
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The backend origin. UNSET IS A VALID, WORKING CONFIGURATION: every path then
# resolves against this origin and is served by the bundled route handlers from
# fixtures. Set it to point the shipped endpoints at a real backend.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:4000

# Set to 1 to send every non-pinned endpoint to the backend, including routes it
# has not shipped yet. Useful when the backend catches up; leave unset otherwise.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_ALL=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The chain
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The JSON-RPC endpoint offered to the wallet in `wallet_addEthereumChain`.
# Absent, the add-chain payload carries no endpoint and the wallet refuses it —
# which is correct, since a guessed host would register a chain whose every call
# fails.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARC_RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.arc.network

# A block-explorer transaction-page prefix, e.g. `https://example/tx/`.
# LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE for Arc Testnet: the host is not confirmed, and a wrong
# one persists in a user's wallet after the demo ends and attaches a confident
# link to nothing. Unset, settlement references degrade to copyable hashes with a
# note naming this variable, and the add-chain payload omits the key entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EXPLORER_TX_BASE=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The contracts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Absent, the write path reports itself unavailable with a sentence naming the
# missing variable. Neither address is written down in the source: `check-copy`
# forbids a 40-character hex literal outside `src/fixtures/`, because an address
# baked into a component outlives its deployment and an approval signed to a
# stale escrow cannot be taken back.

NEXT_PUBLIC_ESCROW_ADDRESS=0x072eC72D690fe7087397fB9Fa3C834Eb688DD7eA
NEXT_PUBLIC_USDC_ADDRESS=0xa0b91E671669DA217bb026f0553ECB371C0AAa48

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NO `NEXT_PUBLIC_` PREFIX, EVER. This is the credential the backend requires on a
# settling request. It is read only in `src/lib/serverEnv.ts`, which carries
# `import 'server-only'` so importing it from a client component fails the build,
# and `scripts/check-bundle.mjs` greps every emitted client chunk for both the
# identifier and — when it is set at build time — the literal value.
#
# Absent, the settlement proxy answers 401 and names this variable. A deployment
# that can judge but not settle is a perfectly sensible way to run the demo.
# ARBITRA_INTERNAL_KEY=
